    "excerpt": "Indian philosophy explains that once we state something ten thousand times \nit becomes a mantra, a thought form that shapes the future.  The word \"mantra\" \nin its Sanskrit derivation is a combination of two words \"man\" (thought) and \"tra\" \n(liberation).  A mantra should be a thought of liberation.  The slogan, mantra, ...",
    "fullText": "376 i THE INVERSE PARANOID — Indian philosophy explains that once we state something ten thousand times \nit becomes a mantra, a thought form that shapes the future.  The word \"mantra\" \nin its Sanskrit derivation is a combination of two words \"man\" (thought) and \"tra\" \n(liberation).  A mantra should be a thought of liberation.  The slogan, mantra, or \nmotto of the inverse paranoid is: \"Today is the best day ever! \" Try saying that \naloud or to yourself ten thousand times!  I have - and the results are revolu-\ntionary.  \nTHE INVERSE PARANOID \nUnderstand the messages of failure and pain.  Failure and pain speak one \nlanguage - they directly communicate to us what kind of change must \ntake place.  They tell you where you have gone wrong and where you have \ngone right.  They chart your course and tell you when and where you are \noff target.  \nThe next time you are faced with a boring, stressful, intense, extreme, \nhilarious or silly situation, say to yourself: \"Today is the best day ever."
