    "fullText": "THE NATURE OF DISEASES — If potatoes, grain meal, rice or meat are boiled long\nenough, we get jelly-like slime (mucus) or paste\nsimilar to the kind that is used by bookbinders and\ncarpenters.  This slimy substance soon turns sour,\nferments, and forms a bed for fungi, molds and\nbacilli.  During digestion, which is nothing else but\na boiling, a combustion, this slime or paste is\ncreated in the same manner, for the blood can use\nonly the converted dextrose sugar transformed\nfrom starch.  The waste matter, this superfluous\nproduct, i. e. , this paste or slime is at first complete-\n'j\ncreted."
