    "fullText": "10. Anamu ( Petiveria alliacea ) — - Famille Botanique :  - Noms Usuels / Synonymes : Anamu - Parties Utilisées : whole herb - Catégorie Thérapeutique & Tropisme : Reins & Émonctoires (Drainage Rénal & Lithiases) - Source Primaire Rain-Tree : [Anamu Monograph on Rain-Tree.com](https://www.rain-tree.com/anamu.htm) - Principes Phytochimiques Actifs :  - Many biologically active compounds have been discovered in anamu  - including flavonoids  - triterpenes  - steroids  - and sulfur compounds  - Anamu contains a novel sulfur compound named dibenzyl trisulfide  - anamu was one of 34 plants identified with active properties against cancer  - Anamu also contains the phytochemicals astilbin - Indications & Cibles Thérapeutiques :  - therefore  - An alkaloid in anamu  - tetrandrine - Mécanismes & Pharmacodynamie (Leslie Taylor) : 1. Actions Principales (Rain-Tree) : Anamu has been documented to lower blood pressure in two animal studies; therefore, anamu is probably contraindicated for people with low blood pressure. An alkaloid in anamu, tetrandrine, has been documented to have various actions on heart function in animals and humans. Those with a heart condition or taking heart medications should consult with their doctor before using this plant. Anamu has demonstrated to be a uterine relaxant and traditionally employed as a childbirth aid. A pregnant woman should use it only under the supervision of a qualified healthcare practitioner. 2. Recherche Clinique & Activité Biologique : The research published on anamu (and the plant chemicals described above) reveals that it has a broad range of therapeutic properties, including antileukemic, antitumorous, and anticancerous activities. In an in vitro study by Italian researchers in 1990, water extracts and ethanol extracts of anamu retarded the growth of leukemia cells and several other strains of cancerous tumor cells. Three yea... 3. Usage Ethnobotanique Traditionnel : In the Amazon rainforest, anamu is used as part of an herbal bath against witchcraft by the Indians and local jungle herbal healers called curanderos . The Ka'apor Indians call it mikur-ka'a (which means opossum herb) and use it for both medicine and magic. The Caribs in Guatemala crush the root and inhale it for sinusitis, and the Ese'Ejas Indians... - Posologie & Modes d'Administration :  - Dosage Standard : reduces pain reduces spasms Whole plant kills bacteria reduces anxiety Infusion: 1/4 to 1/2 cup 2-3 kills cancer cells reduces depression times daily kills fungi lowers blood sugar Capsules: 1-3 g daily reduces inflammation kills insects kills leukemia cells promotes menstruation reduces free radicals sedates prevents tumors increases perspiration kills viruses expels worms kills Candida increases urination enhances immunity educes fever modulates immune function protects brain  - Infusion / Décoction : 1 cuillère à café à soupe de plante séchée par tasse d'eau chaude, infuser 10-15 min.  - Teinture : 2 à 4 ml (environ 30 à 60 gouttes) 2 fois par jour. - Contre-indications & Précautions :  - Anamu has been documented to lower blood pressure in two animal studies; therefore, anamu is probably contraindicated for people with low blood pressure. An alkaloid in anamu, tetrandrine, has been documented to have various actions on heart function in animals and humans. Those with a heart condition or taking heart medications should consult with their doctor before using this plant. Anamu has demonstrated to be a uterine relaxant and traditionally employed as a childbirth aid. A pregnant woman should use it only under the supervision of a qualified healthcare practitioner. - Note Vitaliste : Fiche botanique officielle Rain-Tree issue des recherches du Dr. Leslie Taylor sur la pharmacopée amazonienne.  ---"
