    "fullText": "18. Boldo ( Peumus boldus ) — - Famille Botanique :  - Noms Usuels / Synonymes : Boldo - Parties Utilisées : Feuilles, écorce, racines ou plante entière - Catégorie Thérapeutique & Tropisme : Reins & Émonctoires (Drainage Rénal & Lithiases) - Source Primaire Rain-Tree : [Boldo Monograph on Rain-Tree.com](https://www.rain-tree.com/boldo.htm) - Principes Phytochimiques Actifs :  - Boldo has many biologically active chemicals  - At least 17 alkaloids have been documented thus far  - several of which are believed to be boldo's main active constituents  - In various studies over the years boldine has shown to protect the liver  - to stimulate the production of bile in the liver  - as well as to stimulate digestion  - In other laboratory tests  - boldine has demonstrated diuretic - Indications & Cibles Thérapeutiques :  - Chemicals in boldo may thin the blood  - chronic use  - Do not exceed the recommended dosages - Mécanismes & Pharmacodynamie (Leslie Taylor) : 1. Actions Principales (Rain-Tree) : 1. Boldo has demonstrated abortive properties and caused fetal birth defects in animal studies and therefore should not be used during pregnancy or while breast-feeding. 2. Chemicals in boldo may thin the blood. Those taking blood-thinning medications (such as Warfarin) or those with disorders that have a tendency towards thin blood (such as thrombocytopenia or hemophilia) should not take boldo unless under the supervision of a qualified healthcare practitioner. Boldo has diuretic effects and is contraindicated for long-term, chronic use. 4. Do not exceed the recommended dosages. 2. Recherche Clinique & Activité Biologique : Researchers verified indigenous uses of boldo leaves in the 1950s and 1960s and showed that leaf extracts had diuretic, digestion stimulation, and bile-producing properties in animal studies. Although these properties are attributed largely to the plant chemical boldine, one study with rats indicated that an alcohol extract of boldo leaves was more active than boldine alone. An ethanol extract of ... 3. Usage Ethnobotanique Traditionnel : Indigenous uses of boldo have been widely documented. Legend has it that the medicinal uses of the plant were discovered by chance: a Chilean shepherd noticed that his sheep were healthier, and had fewer liver problems, when they grazed on native boldo plants growing in his fields. Since this discovery the plant has been used by the indigenous peop... - Posologie & Modes d'Administration :  - Dosage Standard : stimulates digestion kills cancer cells Leaves protects liver stimulates Infusion: 1/2 cup 1-2 times daily detoxifies liver reduces gas Tincture: 2-4 ml twice daily stimulates bile moderately laxative Capsules: 1-2 g twice daily supports gallbladder increases urination reduces inflammation relieves pain protects organs expels worms supports heart function kills parasites fights free radicals regenerates bone relieves oxidative stress regenerates nerves enhances memory aids weight loss lowers cholesterol heals & protects nerves lowers blood pressure  - Infusion / Décoction : 1 cuillère à café à soupe de plante séchée par tasse d'eau chaude, infuser 10-15 min.  - Teinture : 2 à 4 ml (environ 30 à 60 gouttes) 2 fois par jour. - Contre-indications & Précautions :  - 1. Boldo has demonstrated abortive properties and caused fetal birth defects in animal studies and therefore should not be used during pregnancy or while breast-feeding. 2. Chemicals in boldo may thin the blood. Those taking blood-thinning medications (such as Warfarin) or those with disorders that have a tendency towards thin blood (such as thrombocytopenia or hemophilia) should not take boldo unless under the supervision of a qualified healthcare practitioner. Boldo has diuretic effects and is contraindicated for long-term, chronic use. 4. Do not exceed the recommended dosages. - Note Vitaliste : Fiche botanique officielle Rain-Tree issue des recherches du Dr. Leslie Taylor sur la pharmacopée amazonienne.  ---"
