    "fullText": "53. Embauba ( Cecropia peltata, palmata ) — - Famille Botanique :  - Noms Usuels / Synonymes : Embauba - Parties Utilisées : Feuilles, écorce, racines ou plante entière - Catégorie Thérapeutique & Tropisme : Reins & Émonctoires (Drainage Rénal & Lithiases) - Source Primaire Rain-Tree : [Embauba Monograph on Rain-Tree.com](https://www.rain-tree.com/cecropia.htm) - Principes Phytochimiques Actifs :  - Embauba is known to contain glycosides  - lipids  - alkaloids  - tannins  - cardenolids  - triterpenes  - polyphenols  - steroids - Indications & Cibles Thérapeutiques :  - It should not be taken during pregnancy  - increasing the strength of cardiac muscle contraction  - Embauba has demonstrated a hypoglycemic effect in animals  - It is contraindicated for persons with hypoglycemia - Mécanismes & Pharmacodynamie (Leslie Taylor) : 1. Actions Principales (Rain-Tree) : 1. Embauba has a traditional use of aiding childbirth and to promote menstruation. It should not be taken during pregnancy. 2. The plant has been reported in animal studies to have cardiotonic properties, increasing the strength of cardiac muscle contraction. Embauba also has demonstrated hypotensive activity in animal studies. Those with low blood pressure or those on medication to lower their blood pressure should seek the advice of a qualified healthcare professional prior to using this plant. 3. Embauba has demonstrated a hypoglycemic effect in animals. It is contraindicated for persons with hypoglycemia. Diabetics should use this plant with caution as blood sugar levels should be monitored closely and diabetic drugs might need adjusting. 2. Recherche Clinique & Activité Biologique : Embauba’s traditional uses for diabetes has been confirmed in two human studies, numerous animals and in vitro studies. A 2007 study which ran for 34 weeks and used a water extract of embauba leaves reported that a significant reduction of glucose was detected after 4 weeks and HbA1c was also significantly reduced after 6 weeks of treatment. The 2004 clinical trial reported that Type 2 diabetic pa... 3. Usage Ethnobotanique Traditionnel : Indian tribes in the Amazon use embauba for its anti-inflammatory properties – typically for rheumatic, kidney and lung inflammations. The leaf is made into a tea and used widely for asthma and other upper respiratory complaints, as well as for diabetes. It also has been used for sores on the mouth and tongue. The Palikur indigenous people of Guyan... - Posologie & Modes d'Administration :  - Dosage Standard : relieves asthma protects brain Leaves reduces spasms increases urination Infusion: 1 cup 2-3 times daily reduces inflammation stimulates menstruation Capsules: 2-3 g twice daily kills bacteria mildly laxative fights free radicals relieves depression relieves oxidative stress relieves pain protects organs reduces anxiety strengthens heart kills fungi lowers blood pressure relaxes muscles balances blood sugar lowers cholesterol lowers triglycerides  - Infusion / Décoction : 1 cuillère à café à soupe de plante séchée par tasse d'eau chaude, infuser 10-15 min.  - Teinture : 2 à 4 ml (environ 30 à 60 gouttes) 2 fois par jour. - Contre-indications & Précautions :  - 1. Embauba has a traditional use of aiding childbirth and to promote menstruation. It should not be taken during pregnancy. 2. The plant has been reported in animal studies to have cardiotonic properties, increasing the strength of cardiac muscle contraction. Embauba also has demonstrated hypotensive activity in animal studies. Those with low blood pressure or those on medication to lower their blood pressure should seek the advice of a qualified healthcare professional prior to using this plant. 3. Embauba has demonstrated a hypoglycemic effect in animals. It is contraindicated for persons with hypoglycemia. Diabetics should use this plant with caution as blood sugar levels should be monitored closely and diabetic drugs might need adjusting. - Note Vitaliste : Fiche botanique officielle Rain-Tree issue des recherches du Dr. Leslie Taylor sur la pharmacopée amazonienne.  ---"
