    "fullText": "66. Guava ( Psidium guajava ) — - Famille Botanique :  - Noms Usuels / Synonymes : Guava - Parties Utilisées : Fruit, leaf, bark - Catégorie Thérapeutique & Tropisme : Reins & Émonctoires (Drainage Rénal & Lithiases) - Source Primaire Rain-Tree : [Guava Monograph on Rain-Tree.com](https://www.rain-tree.com/guava.htm) - Principes Phytochimiques Actifs :  - Guava is rich in tannins  - phenols  - triterpenes  - flavonoids  - essential oils  - saponins  - carotenoids  - lectins - Indications & Cibles Thérapeutiques :  - corn  - squash  - and other cultivated plants  - In fact  - and Indians also employ it for sore throats  - vomiting - Mécanismes & Pharmacodynamie (Leslie Taylor) : 1. Actions Principales (Rain-Tree) : 1. Guava has recently demonstrated cardiac depressant activity and should be used with caution by those on heart medications. 2. Guava fruit has shown to lower blood sugar levels and it should be avoided by people with hypoglycemia. 2. Recherche Clinique & Activité Biologique : The long history of guava's use has led modern-day researchers to study guava extracts. Its traditional use for diarrhea, gastroenteritis and other digestive complaints has been validated in numerous clinical studies. A plant drug has even been developed from guava leaves (standardized to its quercetin content) for the treatment of acute diarrhea. Human clinical trials with the drug indicate its e... 3. Usage Ethnobotanique Traditionnel : Guava may have been domesticated in Peru several thousand years ago; Peruvian archaeological sites have revealed guava seeds found stored with beans, corn, squash, and other cultivated plants. Guava fruit is still enjoyed as a sweet treat by indigenous peoples throughout the rainforest, and the leaves and bark of the guava tree have a long history ... - Posologie & Modes d'Administration :  - Dosage Standard : stops diarrhea depresses CNS Leaves kills bacteria lowers blood pressure Decoction: 1 cup 1-3 kills fungi reduces blood sugar times daily kills yeast constricts blood vessels kills amebas promotes menstruation relieves pain fights free radicals reduces spasms supports heart  - Infusion / Décoction : 1 cuillère à café à soupe de plante séchée par tasse d'eau chaude, infuser 10-15 min.  - Teinture : 2 à 4 ml (environ 30 à 60 gouttes) 2 fois par jour. - Contre-indications & Précautions :  - 1. Guava has recently demonstrated cardiac depressant activity and should be used with caution by those on heart medications. 2. Guava fruit has shown to lower blood sugar levels and it should be avoided by people with hypoglycemia. - Note Vitaliste : Fiche botanique officielle Rain-Tree issue des recherches du Dr. Leslie Taylor sur la pharmacopée amazonienne.  ---"
