    "fullText": "76. Jatoba ( Hymenaea courbaril ) — - Famille Botanique :  - Noms Usuels / Synonymes : Jatoba, jatob&aacute, stinking toe, algarrobo, azucar huayo, jata&iacute, copal, Brazilian copal, courbaril, nazareno, Cayenne copal, demarara copal, gomme animee, pois confiture, guapinol, guapinole, loksi, South American locust - Parties Utilisées : Bark, resin, leaves - Catégorie Thérapeutique & Tropisme : Reins & Émonctoires (Drainage Rénal & Lithiases) - Source Primaire Rain-Tree : [Jatoba Monograph on Rain-Tree.com](https://www.rain-tree.com/jatoba.htm) - Principes Phytochimiques Actifs :  - sesquiterpenes  - flavonoids  - and oligosaccharides  - copaiba  - which is also featured in this book  - Some of these same chemicals occuring in both plants (such as copalic acid  - delta-cadinene  - antibacterial - Indications & Cibles Thérapeutiques :  - In the Amazon  - used in the manufacture of varnishes  - used as a glaze for pottery  - and is employed medicinally  - love potions and in wedding ceremonies  - Although the name Hymenaea is derived from Hymen - Mécanismes & Pharmacodynamie (Leslie Taylor) : 1. Actions Principales (Rain-Tree) : Documenté par Leslie Taylor pour ses principes phytochimiques actifs. 2. Recherche Clinique & Activité Biologique : In addition to its antifungal properties, jatobá also has been documented to have anti-yeast activity against a wide range of organisms including Candida . Other clinical studies have been performed on jatobá since the early 1970s which have shown that it has antimicrobial, molluscicidal (kills/controls snails & slugs), and antibacterial activities, including in vitro actions against such organism... 3. Usage Ethnobotanique Traditionnel : In the Amazon, jatobá's aromatic copal resin is dug up from the base of the tree and burned as incense, used in the manufacture of varnishes, used as a glaze for pottery, and is employed medicinally. Indians in the Amazon have long used the resin in magic rituals, love potions and in wedding ceremonies. Although the name Hymenaea is derived from Hy... - Posologie & Modes d'Administration :  - Dosage Standard : kills fungi reduces spasms Bark kills Candida decogests bronchials Decoction: 1/2 - 1 cup 1-3 kills mold dries secretions times daily increases energy increases urination Tincture: 1-3 ml twice daily kills bacteria protects liver stimulates digestion expels worms mildly laxative fights free radicals  - Infusion / Décoction : 1 cuillère à café à soupe de plante séchée par tasse d'eau chaude, infuser 10-15 min.  - Teinture : 2 à 4 ml (environ 30 à 60 gouttes) 2 fois par jour. - Contre-indications & Précautions :  - Jatobá leaves have been documented to have a hypoglycemic effect and, as such, should be used under practitioner supervision by diabetics. - Note Vitaliste : Fiche botanique officielle Rain-Tree issue des recherches du Dr. Leslie Taylor sur la pharmacopée amazonienne.  ---"
