    "fullText": "82. Macela ( Achyrocline satureoides ) — - Famille Botanique :  - Noms Usuels / Synonymes : Macela - Parties Utilisées : Feuilles, écorce, racines ou plante entière - Catégorie Thérapeutique & Tropisme : Reins & Émonctoires (Drainage Rénal & Lithiases) - Source Primaire Rain-Tree : [Macela Monograph on Rain-Tree.com](https://www.rain-tree.com/macela.htm) - Principes Phytochimiques Actifs :  - Phytochemical analysis of macela  - which began in the mid-1980s  - For example  - macela contains an uncommon polyphenol named callerianin  - It has been documented with antioxidant  - anti-inflammatory  - pain-relieving  - antibacterial - Indications & Cibles Thérapeutiques :  - pregnant women should still refrain from using this plant  - In herbal medicine systems  - the plant is used as a mild sedative  - Natural herb capsules - Mécanismes & Pharmacodynamie (Leslie Taylor) : 1. Actions Principales (Rain-Tree) : 1. This plant has been documented with hypoglycemic effects in an animal study. Diabetics should monitor their blood glucose levels closely until they determine what effect it might have. 2. This plant has a long history of use as a menstrual promoter and regulator and its biological effects during pregnancy have not been studied or reported. While these traditional uses have not been clinically validated, pregnant women should still refrain from using this plant. 3. One study demonstrated barbiturate potentiation activity when a hot water extract of macela was injected in mice; it remains unclear if this effect is evident when taken orally. In herbal medicine systems, the plant is used as a mild sedative. Natural herb capsules, teas or tinctures might potentiate the effects of other sedatives and barbiturates. Use with caution when taking other prescription sedatives and pain-killers. 2. Recherche Clinique & Activité Biologique : Macela has been the subject of western research and many of its long-time uses in herbal medicine have been validated by scientists. In animal studies with mice and rats, macela demonstrated pain-relieving, anti-inflammatory, and smooth-muscle (gastrointestinal) relaxant properties internally without toxicity, in addition to anti-inflammatory and pain-relief actions externally. This may explain wh... 3. Usage Ethnobotanique Traditionnel : In Brazil, the plant is called macela or marcela and it has been used in herbal medicine for many years. Using the entire plant or just its flowers, a tea is prepared with 5 g of dried herb to 1 liter of boiling water. This infusion is used as a natural remedy for nervous colic, epilepsy, nausea, and gastric problems. It is also employed as an anti... - Posologie & Modes d'Administration :  - Dosage Standard : reduces stomach acid prevents ulcers Aerial parts enhances immunity mildly sedative Flowers kills viruses prevents tumors Infusion: 1 cup 2-3 times daily kills bacteria expels worms Capsules: 1-2 g 2-3 times daily relieves pain supports heart Tincture: 2-3 ml twice daily reduces inflammation prevents clogged arteries fights free radicals promotes perspiration relieves oxidative stress regulates menstruation protects organs stimulates bile relaxes muscles reduces spasms balances blood sugar relieves coughing stimulates digestion modulates immune system  - Infusion / Décoction : 1 cuillère à café à soupe de plante séchée par tasse d'eau chaude, infuser 10-15 min.  - Teinture : 2 à 4 ml (environ 30 à 60 gouttes) 2 fois par jour. - Contre-indications & Précautions :  - Consulter un professionnel de santé en cas de grossesse, allaitement ou traitement médical lourd. - Note Vitaliste : Fiche botanique officielle Rain-Tree issue des recherches du Dr. Leslie Taylor sur la pharmacopée amazonienne.  ---"
