    "fullText": "88. Mulateiro ( Calycophyllum spruceanum ) — - Famille Botanique : Rubiaceae - Noms Usuels / Synonymes : ashi, asho, capirona, capirona de bajo, capirona negra, corusicao, escorrega-macaco, firewood tree, mulateiro, mulateiro-da-várzea, naked tree, palo mulato, pau-marfim, pau mulato, pau-mulato-da-várzea, uhuachaunin, haxo, huiso asho, nahua - Parties Utilisées : Feuilles, écorce, racines ou plante entière - Catégorie Thérapeutique & Tropisme : Immunité & Anti-infectieux (Immunomodulateur & Antiviral) - Source Primaire Rain-Tree : [Mulateiro Monograph on Rain-Tree.com](https://www.rain-tree.com/mulaterio.htm) - Principes Phytochimiques Actifs :  - antifungal and insecticidal activity  - The isolated phenols have demonstrated strong antioxidant activity  - which may explain its traditional use to stop the aging process of the skin - Indications & Cibles Thérapeutiques :  - to its many different uses in folkloric medicine  - parasites and fungal infections  - skin depigmentation  - wrinkles and scars  - The resin is used for abscesses  - and skin tumors - Mécanismes & Pharmacodynamie (Leslie Taylor) : 1. Actions Principales (Rain-Tree) : Documenté par Leslie Taylor pour ses principes phytochimiques actifs. 2. Recherche Clinique & Activité Biologique : Only two studies have been published thus far on mulateiro. In 2001, researchers reported that it demonstrated strong antifungal activity in vitro against eleven common skin fungi and yeasts. In 2003, researchers reported the discovery of several new plant chemicals called seco-iridoids and reported that three of them were active against the tropical parasite, Trypansoma cruzi . With these studies... 3. Usage Ethnobotanique Traditionnel : Mulateiro bark is deeply ingrained in the native culture - from being used as an admixture in the Ayahuasca rituals, to its many different uses in folkloric medicine. In the Amazon a poultice made from the bark is used topically in treating cuts, wounds and burns and believed to have antifungal and wound-healing qualities. The Indians also use a te... - Posologie & Modes d'Administration :  - Dosage Standard : kills bacteria stops bleeding Bark kills fungi Decoction: 1/2 cup 2-3 heals wounds times daily fights free radicals Decoction: Applied topically kills parasites kills insects repels insects soothes skin  - Infusion / Décoction : 1 cuillère à café à soupe de plante séchée par tasse d'eau chaude, infuser 10-15 min.  - Teinture : 2 à 4 ml (environ 30 à 60 gouttes) 2 fois par jour. - Contre-indications & Précautions :  - None known. - Note Vitaliste : Fiche botanique officielle Rain-Tree issue des recherches du Dr. Leslie Taylor sur la pharmacopée amazonienne.  ---"
