    "excerpt": "- Famille Botanique :  - Noms Usuels / Synonymes : Piri-piri - Parties Utilisées : Feuilles, écorce, racines ou plante entière - Catégorie Thérapeutique & Tropisme : Immunité & Anti-infectieux (Immunomodulateur & Antiviral) - Source Primaire Rain-Tree : [Piri-piri Monograph on Rain-Tree.com](https://www.rain-tree.com/p...",
    "fullText": "101. Piri-piri ( Cyperus articulatus ) — - Famille Botanique :  - Noms Usuels / Synonymes : Piri-piri - Parties Utilisées : Feuilles, écorce, racines ou plante entière - Catégorie Thérapeutique & Tropisme : Immunité & Anti-infectieux (Immunomodulateur & Antiviral) - Source Primaire Rain-Tree : [Piri-piri Monograph on Rain-Tree.com](https://www.rain-tree.com/piri-piri.htm) - Principes Phytochimiques Actifs :  - Piri-piri contains flavonoids  - polyphenols  - saponins  - tannins  - terpenes and sugars  - Two of these chemicals  - called cyperotundone and alpha-cyperone  - have been reported with antimalarial actions - Indications & Cibles Thérapeutiques :  - The tall stems and/or the rhizomes are dried and powdered  - to facilitate child birth or to induce an abortion  - as a contraceptive  - and for throat cancer  - and used externally to heal wounds and treat snake bite  - and to allay fright and nervousness - Mécanismes & Pharmacodynamie (Leslie Taylor) : 1. Actions Principales (Rain-Tree) : Documenté par Leslie Taylor pour ses principes phytochimiques actifs. 2. Recherche Clinique & Activité Biologique : Some of the more recent research on piri-piri has focused on its traditional uses to treat epilepsy and convulsions. Researchers in Africa have published several studies which suggest that piri-piri can mediate many of the brain chemical reactions which are required in epilepsy and report that the rhizome has anti-epileptic actions. In addition, other laboratory research with animals reports that ... 3. Usage Ethnobotanique Traditionnel : The indigenous Indian tribes of the Amazon region ascribe magical properties to piri-piri. The tall stems and/or the rhizomes are dried and powdered, or are prepared as a tea and used as a good luck charm or a love potion (called a pusanga). Women will cultivate the plant and bathe their children with it to prevent sickness and injury, and give it ... - Posologie & Modes d'Administration :  - Dosage Standard : stops vomiting kills bacteria Rhizome aids digestion kills yeast Infusion: 1 cup twice daily eases diarrhea contraceptive Extract: 2 ml 2-3 times daily relieves gas Capsules: 1-2 g twice daily calms & sedates reduces seizures expels worms prevents convulsions  - Infusion / Décoction : 1 cuillère à café à soupe de plante séchée par tasse d'eau chaude, infuser 10-15 min.  - Teinture : 2 à 4 ml (environ 30 à 60 gouttes) 2 fois par jour. - Contre-indications & Précautions :  - This plant has been traditionally used as a contraceptive aid. While no clinical studies exist to support this traditional use, women seeking to get pregnant should probably avoid the use of this plant. - Note Vitaliste : Fiche botanique officielle Rain-Tree issue des recherches du Dr. Leslie Taylor sur la pharmacopée amazonienne.  ---"
