    "fullText": "124. YERBA MATE (Ilex paraguariensis) Database file in the Tropical Plant Database ( Ilex paraguayiensis ) — - Famille Botanique :  - Noms Usuels / Synonymes : YERBA MATE (Ilex paraguariensis) Database file in the Tropical Plant Database - Parties Utilisées : Leaves - Catégorie Thérapeutique & Tropisme : Reins & Émonctoires (Drainage Rénal & Lithiases) - Source Primaire Rain-Tree : [YERBA MATE (Ilex paraguariensis) Database file in the Tropical Plant Database Monograph on Rain-Tree.com](https://www.rain-tree.com/yerbamate.htm) - Principes Phytochimiques Actifs :  - theobromine  - and theophylline)  - saponins  - and 10% chlorogenic acid  - Sterols resembling ergosterol and cholesterol are also present in yerba mate  - and novel saponins have been discovered in the leaf (and named matesaponins)  - Saponins are plant chemicals with known pharmacological activities  - including - Indications & Cibles Thérapeutiques :  - In the early 16th century  - Juan de Solís  - a Spanish explorer of South America's famed La Plata River  - \" The Spaniards tried the beverage and liked it  - the branches are cut  - then held over an open fire (to fire-cure the leaves) - Mécanismes & Pharmacodynamie (Leslie Taylor) : 1. Actions Principales (Rain-Tree) : Documenté par Leslie Taylor pour ses principes phytochimiques actifs. 2. Recherche Clinique & Activité Biologique : One of the largest areas of research on yerba mate is the confirmation of the plant’s strong antioxidant actions. More than 50 human, animal and test tube studies reveal how yerba mate can reduce or prevent unstable molecules (called free radicals or reactive oxygen species) from harming various cells and organs in the body (called oxidative stress). The damage caused by oxidative stress can be a ... 3. Usage Ethnobotanique Traditionnel : Yerba mate was has been used as a beverage since the time of the ancient Indians of Brazil and Paraguay. In the early 16th century, Juan de Solís, a Spanish explorer of South America's famed La Plata River, reported that the Guarani Indians of Paraguay brewed a leaf tea that \"produced exhilaration and relief from fatigue.\" The Spaniards tried the b... - Posologie & Modes d'Administration :  - Dosage Standard : increases energy relieves pain Leaves promotes weight loss increases bile Infusion: 1 cup 2-3 suppresses appetite mildly laxative times daily burns fat protects liver Capsules: 1-2 g twice daily reduces inflammation reduces blood pressure modulates immune system enhances immunity prevents chronic diseases protects brain protects heart relieves depression fights free radicals reduces blood sugar avoids diabetic complications protects bones relieves oxidative stress enhances memory  - Infusion / Décoction : 1 cuillère à café à soupe de plante séchée par tasse d'eau chaude, infuser 10-15 min.  - Teinture : 2 à 4 ml (environ 30 à 60 gouttes) 2 fois par jour. - Contre-indications & Précautions :  - Yerba mate contains caffeine and should not be used by those who are sensitive or allergic to caffeine. Excessive consumption of caffeine is contraindicated for persons with high blood pressure, diabetes, ulcers, and other diseases. Yerba mate tea should not be consumed excessively and chronically (as it has been linked to possibly increasing the risk of certain such cancers as oral and esophageal cancer). Taking yerba mate in capsules instead of hot teas avoids this possible risk. Yerba mate has been reported to have MAO-inhibitor activity. Those persons taking MAO-inhibitor drugs should use yerba mate with caution to monitor these possible effects. Yerba mate has been reported to lower blood pressure in human studies. Those taking medications to lower blood pressure should use with caution until they determine if medications need adjusting. Yerba mate has been reported to lower blood glucose levels in human studies. Diabetics should consult with their health care provider to determine if diabetes medications need adjusting while taking yerba mate. - Note Vitaliste : Fiche botanique officielle Rain-Tree issue des recherches du Dr. Leslie Taylor sur la pharmacopée amazonienne.  ---"
