    "fullText": "126. Yacon (Smallanthus sonchifolius) Database file in the Tropical Plant Database for herbal remedies ( Smallanthus sonchifolius ) — - Famille Botanique : Asteraceae - Noms Usuels / Synonymes : aricoma, arboloco, aricona, arikuma, colla, chiriguano, ipio, jac&oacute, n, jicama, jiquima, jikima, jiquimilla, leafcup, llacon, llacoma, mexican potato, polaco, poire de terre, potato bean, puhe, shicama, taraca, yac&oacute, n, yacuma, yacumpi - Parties Utilisées : root, leaves - Catégorie Thérapeutique & Tropisme : Reins & Émonctoires (Drainage Rénal & Lithiases) - Source Primaire Rain-Tree : [Yacon (Smallanthus sonchifolius) Database file in the Tropical Plant Database for herbal remedies Monograph on Rain-Tree.com](https://www.rain-tree.com/yacon.htm) - Principes Phytochimiques Actifs :  - These compounds help gives the tuber its sweet flavor however  - For this reason  - These oligofructans have been classified as \"prebiotics  - \" Since they are not digested in the human gastrointestinal tract  - In addition to these sweet compounds  - yacon tubers also contain free fructose  - glucose and sucrose as well as inulin and starch  - Both the tuber and the leaves of the plant contain chlorogenic - Indications & Cibles Thérapeutiques :  - In early evolutionary periods  - In South American herbal medicine systems  - hepatosis  - and nephrosis in Bolivia  - In Peru  - Over the last 15 or so years - Mécanismes & Pharmacodynamie (Leslie Taylor) : 1. Actions Principales (Rain-Tree) : Documenté par Leslie Taylor pour ses principes phytochimiques actifs. 2. Recherche Clinique & Activité Biologique : Yacon root contains a high concentration of substances called fructooligosaccharides (FOS) and inulin, which are types of prebiotic soluble fibers. FOS are not digested in the upper gastrointestinal tract, so they don’t spike blood glucose levels. They reach the colon intact and feed specific beneficial gut bacteria which improves insulin sensitivity. Several species of bacteria are responsible fo... 3. Usage Ethnobotanique Traditionnel : In early evolutionary periods, Andean farmers had already recognized the value of yacon as a cultivated food crop. Yacon has been discovered in burial grounds from centuries before the Incas. The first written record of yacon was in 1615 when Felipe Guaman Poma de Ayala included it in a list of 55 native crops cultivated by the Andeans. In South Am... - Posologie & Modes d'Administration :  - Dosage Standard : lowers blood sugar kills bacteria Root fights free radicals kills fungi Powder or Syrup relieves oxidative stress lowers cholesterol follow labeled instructions feeds friendly gut bacteria lowers triglycerides protect brain protects organs protect digestive tract relieves depression promotes weight loss prevents chronic diseases  - Infusion / Décoction : 1 cuillère à café à soupe de plante séchée par tasse d'eau chaude, infuser 10-15 min.  - Teinture : 2 à 4 ml (environ 30 à 60 gouttes) 2 fois par jour. - Contre-indications & Précautions :  - The leaves have demonstrated the ability to significantly lower blood sugar levels. Diabetics should check with a practitioner before using the leaves and monitor their blood sugar levels accordingly as medications may need adjustment. Overconsumption of the root can lead to bloating, gas, or diarrhea due to its high FOS content. People with IBS or FODMAP sensitivities should introduce yacon slowly or consult a healthcare provider. - Note Vitaliste : Fiche botanique officielle Rain-Tree issue des recherches du Dr. Leslie Taylor sur la pharmacopée amazonienne.  ---"
